# Multiplayer (optional). Copy to `.env` and set the game-server URL. When this
# is ABSENT the Multiplayer menu is hidden and the solo game is unaffected —
# there is no runtime network dependency.
#
# Phase 0 replaced the old P2P stack (Supabase Realtime + WebRTC mesh + TURN)
# with a single authoritative Node game server (see the `server/` package). Run
# it locally with `npm run server` and point the client at it:
VITE_GAME_SERVER_URL=ws://localhost:8787
#
# MULTI-REGION (optional): to offer players regions, set a LIST as JSON. Each entry
# needs {id, label, region, url}. The client pings every one (/health) so the player
# picks the lowest latency, and the choice is remembered on their account.
# VITE_GAME_SERVERS takes precedence over VITE_GAME_SERVER_URL.
#
#   MODEL M (recommended) — ONE Fly app, one machine per region. All entries share
#   the SAME base url (the app's Anycast hostname); the client adds a ?region= /
#   ?mm= / ?room= hint and the server fly-replays the connection to the right
#   region. Region-local ranked works via Anycast; cross-region ranked/rooms via
#   fly-replay. Deploy: one `fly deploy`, then `fly scale count 1 --region <code>`.
# VITE_GAME_SERVERS=[{"id":"iad","label":"US East","region":"iad","url":"wss://dohun-sim-decode.fly.dev"},{"id":"sjc","label":"US West","region":"sjc","url":"wss://dohun-sim-decode.fly.dev"},{"id":"lhr","label":"Europe","region":"lhr","url":"wss://dohun-sim-decode.fly.dev"},{"id":"syd","label":"Oceania","region":"syd","url":"wss://dohun-sim-decode.fly.dev"},{"id":"nrt","label":"Asia","region":"nrt","url":"wss://dohun-sim-decode.fly.dev"}]
#
#   MODEL S (legacy) — separate app/URL per region (each entry a distinct url).
# VITE_GAME_SERVERS=[{"id":"us","label":"US East","region":"iad","url":"wss://decode-iad.fly.dev"},{"id":"eu","label":"Europe","region":"lhr","url":"wss://decode-lhr.fly.dev"}]
#
# Each server reports its region via SERVER_REGION (or Fly's FLY_REGION, set
# automatically) — surfaced on /health (x-region header) and /api/presence.
#
# MATCHMAKER_REGION (server env, default 'iad'): the region that holds the global
# ranked queue. All ?mm=1 connections fly-replay here; keep min_machines_running=1
# in this region (fly.toml) so it's always warm. Cross-region ranked also needs
# DATABASE_URL (the paired roster is staged in Postgres for the host machine).
# MATCHMAKER_REGION=iad
#
# In production, deploy `server/` (e.g. Fly.io) and use its wss:// URL:
# VITE_GAME_SERVER_URL=wss://your-decode-server.fly.dev
#
# On Vercel: add VITE_GAME_SERVER_URL under Project → Settings → Environment
# Variables (baked in at build time — redeploy after a change). For Electron, set
# it in the shell before `npm run dist`.
#
# ALPHA PREVIEW: the `alpha` branch has its OWN game server and its own database, so
# preview features that write to Postgres (standing, dodge penalties, reports, playtime,
# ranked) can actually be tested instead of silently no-opping. Scope these to Vercel's
# PREVIEW environment / the alpha branch — see docs/deploy.md:
#   VITE_GAME_SERVER_URL=wss://dsim-alpha.fly.dev
#   VITE_APP_CHANNEL=alpha
#
# SERVER SIDE (a Fly env var, not a client one — set in fly.alpha.toml, not here):
#   SERVER_CHANNEL=alpha   ⇒ this deployment PERSISTS alpha results, to its own database.
# Absent/'stable' ⇒ the production behaviour: an alpha build's results are never written,
# because on a shared server they would land in production's boards.

# RELEASE CHANNEL of this client build. Default 'stable'. Set to 'alpha' on the
# PRE-RELEASE deployment (e.g. the alpha Vercel project). The server then (a)
# matchmakes alpha players ONLY with other alpha players — they run a different
# src/sim, so mixing channels in one authoritative match desyncs — and (b) does
# NOT persist alpha results to the leaderboard/ELO DB. Stable/omitted ⇒ normal.
# VITE_APP_CHANNEL=alpha

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase 3 — leaderboards / records / ranked ELO (SERVER-SIDE only).
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The game server persists verified scores + replays to Neon Postgres. When
# DATABASE_URL is ABSENT the server still runs matches — only leaderboards/
# records/ELO are disabled. NEVER put these in the client build (VITE_*): the
# server is the only trusted writer.
#
# Neon connection string (includes ?sslmode=require). Set as a Fly SECRET, not
# a build var:  fly secrets set DATABASE_URL='postgres://…' -a dohun-sim-decode
DATABASE_URL=
# optional pool size (default 5)
# DB_POOL_MAX=5
#
# Neon Auth (identity — Better Auth based). ONE client var: the hosted auth
# endpoint on your Neon project, found in the Neon dashboard → Auth tab (looks
# like https://ep-xxx.neonauth.<region>.aws.neon.build/<db>/auth). Safe to
# expose (public). Client SDK: `@neondatabase/auth` → createAuthClient(URL).
# Identity syncs into the Postgres `neon_auth.users_sync` table, so the server
# (which already has DATABASE_URL) attributes runs by joining on the user id.
VITE_NEON_AUTH_URL=

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Advertising (Google AdSense). OPTIONAL — absent means NO ads anywhere, no
# script is loaded, and the ad columns never render.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTE: index.html ALSO hardcodes the adsbygoogle script tag with the publisher id,
# so the AdSense crawler finds it on every route of this client-rendered SPA. If you
# set VITE_ADSENSE_CLIENT it MUST match that tag - the build FAILS on a mismatch,
# because ads.txt authorizing one seller while the tag requests as another is exactly
# the drift ads.txt exists to catch. The Electron build strips the tag (serving in an
# app wrapper is a policy violation).
#
# VITE_ADSENSE_CLIENT is issued at AdSense SIGNUP, before approval - set it as
# soon as you have it. On its own it shows NO ads (every unit also needs its own
# slot id, and you cannot create ad units until you are approved); what it does is
# emit /ads.txt and the <meta name="google-adsense-account"> verification tag, which
# is exactly what the review needs to confirm you own the site.
#
# Set the SLOT ids only AFTER approval. Approval also requires a live privacy
# policy (served at /privacy) and takes days to weeks.
#
# Publisher id from the AdSense dashboard.
# VITE_ADSENSE_CLIENT=ca-pub-0000000000000000
#
# Per-unit slot ids, also from the dashboard. A unit with no slot id stays off,
# so you can enable the menu units before the in-game ones (or vice versa).
#   GAME = the 160x600 skyscrapers flanking the field during a match.
#   MENU = a 300x250 rectangle on the shell pages.
# VITE_ADSENSE_SLOT_GAME=0000000000
# VITE_ADSENSE_SLOT_MENU=0000000000
#
#   RESULTS = a 300x250 on the post-match results screen.
# VITE_ADSENSE_SLOT_RESULTS=0000000000
#
# Ads are suppressed unconditionally in three cases, regardless of these vars:
# the Electron desktop build (AdSense forbids serving in an app wrapper), touch
# devices (no free space — the field would shrink), and supporters.
#
# /ads.txt is GENERATED at build time from VITE_ADSENSE_CLIENT (see vite.config.ts).
# There is nothing to commit and nothing to keep in sync.
#
# ---- audience signals -------------------------------------------------------
# Ads are NON-PERSONALIZED by default and tagged as being for users below the age
# of consent. That is deliberate, not an oversight: DSIM simulates FIRST Tech
# Challenge (grades 7-12), the sim is fully playable signed out, and most
# impressions therefore have no age signal at all. Set these only with a reason.
#
#   VITE_ADSENSE_PERSONALIZED=1  allow behavioural targeting (default: off)
#   VITE_ADSENSE_TFUAC=0         stop tagging under-age-of-consent (default: on)
#   VITE_ADSENSE_TFCD=1          also assert COPPA child-directed (default: off —
#                                the terms set a 13+ minimum, so this would be an
#                                inaccurate declaration rather than a cautious one)
#
# CONSENT: Google Funding Choices loads automatically whenever VITE_ADSENSE_CLIENT
# is set — a Google-certified CMP is REQUIRED for EEA/UK/Swiss traffic, and without
# one Google serves those users no ads at all. The message itself is authored in the
# AdSense dashboard under Privacy & messaging; there is no key to configure here.
#
# BEFORE ENABLING VITE_ADSENSE_SLOT_GAME: the in-game columns sit beside a 60 Hz
# canvas. Load a match with `?perf=1`, drive for ~10s with the columns off, then
# again with them on, and compare p95 frame time. The menu/results units carry no
# such risk and can ship first.

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supporter memberships (Ko-fi). SERVER-side — these are NOT VITE_ vars.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The shared token Ko-fi sends in every webhook payload. With it unset the
# webhook endpoint is CLOSED (503) rather than open — an unauthenticated grant
# path is worse than no grant path. Set as a Fly secret and point the Ko-fi
# webhook at https://<app>.fly.dev/api/kofi/webhook
# KOFI_VERIFICATION_TOKEN=
#
# What a payment BUYS. A subscription payment is always one month; a one-off tip
# buys floor(amount / price) months, capped. A payment in another currency grants
# nothing rather than guessing an exchange rate.
# KOFI_MONTHLY_PRICE=3
# KOFI_CURRENCY=USD
# KOFI_MAX_MONTHS=24

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Analytics (Vercel Web Analytics). OPTIONAL, off by default.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cookieless and stores no personal data, so it needs no consent banner. Without
# it there is no way to tell whether the ads or the membership actually work.
# VITE_ANALYTICS=1

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supporter tier visibility. OFF by default.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gates the Support page and its footer link. The tier's code is complete, but
# the page must not go live before the Ko-fi page exists AND the server has
# KOFI_VERIFICATION_TOKEN: otherwise the "Support on Ko-fi" button leads
# somewhere that cannot take a payment and every claim fails. A broken purchase
# path is also a cited AdSense rejection reason.
# VITE_SUPPORT_ENABLED=1

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Name moderation (SERVER-SIDE only). Automated profanity/slur filtering for
# user-supplied names: account username, display name, and the robot/team names
# that land on the public leaderboard. When MODERATION_API_KEY is ABSENT the
# filter is DISABLED and every name is allowed (like DATABASE_URL gates records)
# — the solo/local build is unaffected. The server is the authority; the client
# only shows a hint. NEVER put the key in the client build (VITE_*): the key must
# not ship to browsers, and the provider would reject a CORS call anyway.
#
# Set as a Fly SECRET:  fly secrets set MODERATION_API_KEY='sk-…' -a dohun-sim-decode
#
# Defaults call OpenAI's FREE Moderation endpoint (omni-moderation-latest). Point
# MODERATION_API_URL/MODEL at any provider with the same {input}→{results:[{flagged}]}
# shape to swap it. Fails OPEN on an outage (allows the name, logs a warning); the
# admin console (/admin) forced-rename is the human backstop.
# MODERATION_API_KEY=
# MODERATION_API_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1/moderations
# MODERATION_MODEL=omni-moderation-latest
# MODERATION_TIMEOUT_MS=4000

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SELF-HOSTED LAN PLAY — the FEATURE GATE. Two halves, and both are needed.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAN play is finished and ships on the ALPHA channel; it is deliberately held
# back from production for now. Both halves FAIL CLOSED, so an environment that
# sets neither behaves exactly like one where the feature does not exist.
#
#   VITE_LAN_ENABLED=1   CLIENT (a Vercel env var, baked in at build time).
#                        Shows the Play screen's LAN tile, the /lan panel, the
#                        "unofficial — not ranked" banner, and the self-hosted
#                        rows on Career. Cosmetic: it hides doors, it does not
#                        lock them. See src/net/env.ts.
#
#   LAN_UPLOADS=1        SERVER (a Fly env var — it is in fly.alpha.toml, NOT in
#                        fly.toml). Decides whether `/api/lan` is mounted at all,
#                        i.e. whether this deployment accepts a self-hosted match
#                        and its replay. This is the half that actually closes the
#                        door. See server/lanUploads.ts.
#
# Turning LAN on for an environment means setting BOTH, on the client project and
# on the game server it talks to. The client flag alone leaves a visible button
# whose uploads 404; the server flag alone leaves an open route nobody can see.
#
#   LAN_SIGNALLING=1     SERVER, and the THIRD door. Decides whether browsers may claim a
#                        room code and be introduced to each other for TAB HOSTING
#                        (docs/lan-webrtc.md). It is SEPARATE from LAN_UPLOADS because the
#                        rendezvous touches no database at all — which is exactly why the
#                        upload gate does not cover it. Also in fly.alpha.toml only.
#
# TO TEST TAB HOSTING BETWEEN TWO MACHINES WITH NOTHING DEPLOYED: `npm run lan:tab` sets all
# of this up on your own machine and prints the URL both machines open. Nobody is signed in
# there, so the match stays on the device until it is played against a real server.
# VITE_LAN_ENABLED=1

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SELF-HOSTED (LAN) SERVER. Both of these, or neither.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# (UNRELATED to the two flags above. These are what somebody's own laptop sets to
# BECOME a LAN server; the flags above are what a CLOUD deployment sets to expose
# the feature. A LAN server needs neither of them.)
# A LAN server is somebody's laptop hosting a game for the room it is in
# (docs/lan-selfhost.md). The desktop app sets both of these itself when you
# press Host, so you need them only to run `node dist-server/lan.mjs` by hand.
#
# LAN_MODE=1 is what makes the server enforce the policy ON ITSELF: it refuses
# to build a database pool even with DATABASE_URL set, never verifies anybody's
# credentials, and drops the admin environment. Nothing about a LAN match can
# reach a board, a rank or an ELO, whoever started the process and however.
#
# SERVE_CLIENT points at a built `dist/`, which the host serves to the LAN over
# plain HTTP on the same origin as the WebSocket. That is not a convenience: a
# browser refuses a ws:// socket from an https:// page as mixed content, so a
# guest cannot reach a LAN server any other way.
#
# ⚠️ SERVE_CLIENT WITHOUT LAN_MODE REFUSES TO START. Serving the client is the
# one thing only a self-hosted server does, and one that could also reach the
# cloud database would be a machine its operator can patch holding write access
# to the real boards. The pair is one decision; failing closed is deliberate,
# because the dangerous case is somebody who thought they were starting an
# ordinary server.
# LAN_MODE=1
# SERVE_CLIENT=/path/to/dist
